<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gang Gam-chan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/948">948</a>–<a href="/years/1031">1031</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kang Kam-chan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gang Gam-chan was a Korean scholar-official and general of the Goryeo dynasty. As commander against the third Khitan invasion, he destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gwiju">Battle of Gwiju</a> in 1019, a victory that secured Goryeo's independence. He is honoured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> as one of its great national heroes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gang-gam-chan.jpg" alt="Gang Gam-chan" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 공공누리 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%EA%B0%95%EA%B0%90%EC%B0%AC_%EC%9E%A5%EA%B5%B0_%ED%9D%89%EC%83%81.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kang was born on 22 December 948 into a prominent aristocratic family of the Geumju Kang clan, in Geumju, now Gwanak District in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. His father had served King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Taejo of Goryeo</a> and was honoured for helping to found the dynasty. A legend tells that a meteor fell towards the house on the day of his birth, and the place is still called Nakseongdae, site of the falling star. From the age of seven he learned Confucian philosophy, military tactics and martial arts from his father, and after his father died in 964 he travelled around the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 983 Kang took the top score in the civil service examination, and in 992 he joined the court as a deputy under the Minister of Education. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> general Xiao Paiya invaded in 993 he supported negotiation, and the talks of Sŏ Hŭi brought a truce. After General Kang Cho murdered King Mokjong in 1009, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shengzong-of-liao">Emperor Shengzong of Liao</a> invaded in 1010 with, by the figure given, 400,000 soldiers. Kang urged King Hyeonjong to flee rather than surrender, and the king escaped the burning capital; resistance in the country then forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> to withdraw.</p><p>When Xiao Baiya led 100,000 men into Goryeo in 1018, many officials wanted peace talks, but Kang advised war, since this army was smaller than earlier ones. Serving as acting deputy War minister, he led about 208,000 men towards the border, won the Battle of Heunghwajin by breaking a dam as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a> crossed the stream, and in 1019 caught the starving, retreating enemy at Gwiju, where only Xiao and a few others escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kang retired from the army and the government in 1020, was appointed Supreme Chancellor in 1030 and died on 9 September 1031; he was buried in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheongju">Cheongju</a>. His victories are often compared with those of General Ŭlchi Mundŏk at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salsu">Battle of Salsu</a> and Admiral Yi Sun-sin at Hansan and Myeongnyang. The peace that followed lasted about a century, a balance between Goryeo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> grew strong. His shrine, Anguksa, stands in Sadang-dong, Gwanak District.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kang Kamch'an” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Khitan returned in 1018, the commander <b>Gang Gam-chan</b> destroyed their army at the Battle of Gwiju in 1019; according to the Goryeosa only a few thousand of the invaders escaped.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nakseongdae, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.47,126.96&amp;year=1001" aria-label="Nakseongdae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7724 815 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gang Gam-chan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8174 1085h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8174 1085h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.47,126.96&amp;year=1001">What was here in 1001? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
